---
{{ hash_banner() }}
#
# What MoveIt is allowed to ask for, as a fraction of the limits declared in the
# robot description. The description's limits are the arm's; these are the
# planner's.
#
# cross-cutting-safety.md requires limits enforced at planning AND at execution.
# This file is the planning half. The execution half is NOT symmetric with it,
# and saying so is the point of this paragraph: an earlier version of it read
# "this file is the planning half, and the controller is the other". That was
# already untrue for the acceleration ceiling, and the deceleration ceiling made
# it untrue for a second quantity.
#
# What the controller actually enforces. `enforce_command_limits: true` in the
# generated controller configuration builds its limiter from the URDF `<limit>`
# element, which carries position, velocity and effort and has NO field for
# acceleration or deceleration.
#
# Half of what this paragraph used to say is now out of date, and the half that
# is not is the important one. It read: "The generated
# `JointTrajectoryController` configuration declares no `constraints:` block at
# all, and `joint_trajectory_controller` defaults every tolerance in one to 0.0,
# which means disabled." That block now exists — ADR-0036 generates `goal_time`
# and per-joint `trajectory` and `goal` tolerances from the arm type, so a
# trajectory the controller fails to TRACK is detected and aborted where it
# previously reported SUCCEEDED.
#
# What has NOT changed is the sentence that follows, and no part of ADR-0036
# should be read as closing it. Those tolerances bound POSITION ERROR — how far
# the arm is from where the trajectory said it would be. They say nothing about
# how hard it accelerated or braked getting there. So the ACCELERATION and
# DECELERATION ceilings below are still stated here and enforced nowhere at
# execution: a trajectory that respects them is planned, and nothing checks that
# what runs still does. A deceleration ceiling the physical arm cannot honour is
# still caught by nothing in this repository, on either backend.
#
# That remains a real gap, it is named rather than implied (P7), and closing it
# needs a limit the URDF has no field to express — not a tolerance.
default_velocity_scaling_factor: {{ arm.max_velocity_scaling | f }}
default_acceleration_scaling_factor: {{ arm.max_acceleration_scaling | f }}

# Only the acceleration limit is stated here. Velocity and effort limits are in
# the robot description, which is where a limit belongs — it is a property of the
# arm. Repeating them would be the same fact in two places, and declaring
# `has_velocity_limits` without a value makes MoveIt refuse to load the model
# ("specified a velocity limit ... but did not set a max velocity").
#
# Acceleration is the exception: the vendor description carries no acceleration
# limit, and MoveIt's time parameterisation needs one. Without it the planner
# emits trajectories the controller cannot track, which presents as following
# error rather than as a missing limit.
#
# Deceleration is the second exception and it arrived with ADR-0027. Pilz's
# trapezoidal profile brakes on its own ceiling rather than on the acceleration
# one, and refuses to build a PTP generator for a group whose joints do not all
# have one: "deceleration limit not set for group <name>", raised at PLAN time
# rather than at load time.
#
# Stated precisely, because an earlier version of this paragraph was wrong about
# it: omitting this key would NOT have produced that refusal. MoveIt derives the
# ceiling first — `pilz_industrial_motion_planner/src/joint_limits_aggregator.cpp`
# does `if (has_acceleration_limits && !has_deceleration_limits) {
# max_deceleration = -max_acceleration; has_deceleration_limits = true; }`,
# present in tags 2.10, 2.12, 2.13 and on jazzy HEAD — and
# `has_acceleration_limits` is already true above, so -2.0 would have been
# derived: exactly the value emitted here. The refusal needs the ACCELERATION
# limit to be absent as well.
#
# The key is stated anyway, and this is the reason that survives the correction:
# it makes the braking ceiling a decision about this arm instead of an accident
# of its acceleration ceiling. Derived, the two can never differ, and the day an
# arm has to brake harder than it accelerates the model has nowhere to say so.
# MoveIt's convention is that the value is negative; the model states a
# magnitude, and the sign is applied here, where MoveIt is the consumer.
joint_limits:
{% for joint in arm.joints %}
  {{ joint }}:
    has_acceleration_limits: true
    max_acceleration: {{ arm.max_acceleration_rad_s2 | f }}
    has_deceleration_limits: true
    max_deceleration: {{ -arm.max_deceleration_rad_s2 | f }}
{% endfor %}
